The bitfield clear mask was zero, so every write wiped the whole unit.

    int unit_bits = bf_size * 8;
    int bf_clear = (~(bf_mask << bf_off)) & ((1 << unit_bits) - 1);

A shift count is taken modulo the register width. unit_bits is 64 -- micro-c
makes `int` and `unsigned` eight bytes, so the storage unit is eight bytes for
essentially every bitfield there is -- and `1 << 64` is therefore 1, not
2^64. `(1 << 64) - 1` is 0. The emitted store became

    mov_x0,0        # bitfield: clear mask
    and_x0,x1,x0    # the whole unit, gone
    orr_x0,x1,x0

so writing ONE bitfield destroyed every other field sharing its unit. This
fired on every bitfield write in every program; it was not a corner case.

WHAT IT COST. tcc keeps function attributes exactly in that shape:

    struct FuncAttr { unsigned func_call:3, func_type:2, func_noreturn:1,
                      func_ctor:1, func_dtor:1, func_args:8, ... };

Setting the calling convention cleared func_ctor and the rest. gen_function
then branches on what survived:

    put_extern_sym(sym, cur_text_section, ind, 0);
    if (sym->type.ref->f.func_ctor)
        add_array(tcc_state, ".init_array", sym->c);

which is the statement immediately after the last marker the instrumented run
reported, for the symbol it named:

    LAST STATEMENT THAT COMPLETED: P69
      put_extern_sym: put_extern_sym2(sym, s ? s->sh_num : SHN_UNDEF, ...)
    PES-enter  sym->v=1376  type.t=6 (VT_FUNC)  r=560 (VT_CONST|VT_SYM)
               sh_num=1  name=g

-- `g`, the function being DEFINED, not `f`, the undefined one that the probe
table had pointed at. The trigger was "a call to a declared-but-undefined
function" because that is what makes tcc reach gen_function with a relocation
already emitted.

A FULL-WIDTH UNIT NEEDS NO MASKING. ~(mask << off) is already exactly the bits
to keep; the & was only ever there to trim a narrower unit, and it is now
applied only when the unit is narrower.

WHY A TWO-COLUMN SUITE NEVER CAUGHT IT. The bitfield write path emits aarch64
mnemonics literally --

    emit_out("and_x0,x1,x0\n");

-- so on amd64 it does not assemble at all: `Received invalid other`. Every
bitfield write case would have failed to build on the reference column, so
there were none. A suite only finds bugs in the shapes it can express, and one
column could not express this shape at all. difftest.sh now supports a case
declaring AARCH64 ONLY, skipped on the other column WITH THE REASON PRINTED --
never silently, because a silent skip is how a suite quietly stops testing
something.

ALSO FIXED HERE: `mov_rbx,r15` does not exist in amd64_defs.M1 either, so the
value now travels through the accumulator, the same routing the author already
used for aarch64's missing mov_x16,x1. That is not enough to make bitfield
writes portable -- the hardcoded mnemonics remain -- but it removes one of two
reasons the construct cannot assemble there, and the remaining one is recorded.

MEASURED

    A/B on the fix   without: case 73 fails (first field reads wrong)
                     with:    passes
    difftest         71 pass / 0 fail / 1 known gap / 1 arch-skipped, amd64
                     72 pass / 0 fail / 1 known gap, aarch64
    corpus           423 of 426 on aarch64

